| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |
| C-ID | One independently assessable statement | User statement, external fact, observation, inference, proposal, or assumption | Source version and location or actual record | Question, method, and pre-established decision criterion | Limit the conclusion to the evidence | Goal, requirement, feature, acceptance, or issue IDs |

| Decision ID | Explicit user wording and original location | Stage and object versions | Scope and conditions | Confirmation action and time | Validity and impact of change |
| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |
| D-ID | Original wording as the authoritative basis | Stage snapshot and R/F/A or other object IDs | Project, platform, permissions, and cost bounds | Verifiable user action | Effective, awaiting reconfirmation, or superseded, with the new version linked |

### 2.3 Status and evidence

| Object | Status | Evidence required |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Product content | Draft, pending decision, stage confirmed, or recheck needed | Drafts may be reviewed and exported with their status visible. Use only confirmed versions as confirmed execution baselines. |
| Source material | Candidate, adopted, rejected, or withdrawn, with separate version applicability | Track adoption, factual support, applicability, and stage confirmation independently. |
| PC or KB document check | Satisfied, unsatisfied, unverified, or not applicable with rationale | A populated field supports a structural check. Semantic findings require evidence of the content. |
| Research or prototype | Planned, unexecuted, or executed with actual results and limitations | Record real participant research only when it occurred. Attribute model simulation and method references as such. |
| Application test | Passed, failed, unverified, or not applicable with rationale | Bind the result to the actual artifact, environment, action, and evidence. Unimplemented behavior remains unverified where applicable. |
| Issue | Open, investigating, awaiting user decision, environment blocked, revising, awaiting recheck, or closed | Close against the original condition with the required evidence. Preserve status through planning, pause, or handoff. |

## 3. Product stages, clarification, and confirmation

Initialization, discovery, modeling, and planning describe the maturity of the product content. For standalone documentation, the current policy uses scope confirmation and final confirmation of the concrete document as the primary review points. Show the current version, changes, impacts, and open items at each intermediate stage. Mark unconfirmed content as draft. Follow a user's explicit stage-by-stage approval request and obtain any decision required by a material change of scope, design, permissions, or cost.

For work in an existing development run, use its saved workflow stage, mode, decision contract, and entry conditions for progression. In a guided run, record actual confirmation for the version and stage the workflow presents for review. Document confirmation applies to the version reviewed. Execution, application acceptance, and release follow their own authorization and readiness conditions.

The frozen design required effective user confirmation at each named product stage. Apply that requirement when it is an effective decision for the current project. Reuse valid earlier decisions within their scope and verify the required prior-stage baseline before relying on it. Preserve earlier confirmed versions when a later revision is proposed.

### 3.1 Clarify before formal execution

If a new project still needs a decision on planning support, ask whether the user wants suggestions on uncertain directions. Provide selectable options and a free-text answer. The selection determines how suggestions are presented. Technical stack choices and execution approval have their own decisions.

Group the known initial questions under the common contract. Give each question an independent answer and record any dependency between questions. Reuse valid answers. When a new question, conflict, or boundary arises later, ask one material question at a time and explain the impact. Missing answers, preselected options, and timeouts leave the decision pending. Continue independent preparation when it is authorized and useful.

| Question field | Record |
| --- | --- |
| Question ID and stage | Q-ID and stage |
| Known facts and original wording | Source and decision versions with original locations |
| Decision needed | One answerable question |
| Impact on understanding | Goals, behavior, data, permissions, or costs affected |
| Selectable options | Option ID, short label, resulting behavior, main tradeoff, and any recommendation. Use two or three mutually exclusive choices when appropriate. |
| Free-text answer | A way for the user to specify their own choice and details |
| Actual user choice or wording | Observable action or original answer, with unanswered items left pending |
| Resulting changes | Affected objects, versions, and differences |
| Further confirmation | Clarification, source adoption, stage confirmation, or permission decision as separate actions |

For a planning-support question, options might include “Offer suggestions one decision at a time” and “Start by organizing my description,” alongside a free-text answer. A stage review where required identifies the exact version, scope, and differences and gives the user an explicit way to confirm, request revision, or defer.

Once the direction is clear, examine applicable boundaries such as first and subsequent use, repeated actions, invalid or empty inputs, cancellation or interruption, recovery, data loss or overwrite, permissions, external transmission, cost, platform differences, source changes, and conflicts. Ask about boundaries that affect the project. Include recovery, undo, accounts, or billing only when the requirements or actual behavior warrant them.

The frozen source records a user's requirement of at least 90% confidence in understanding before formal execution. Under current policy, describe the confirmed goals, required tasks, scope, material tradeoffs, boundaries, and the impact of remaining unknowns. If an estimate is recorded, include its basis and the unresolved points. Apply an explicit confidence threshold where the current user requires it. An estimate expresses a reviewable judgment about understanding. Statistical evidence, actual user confirmation, and execution authority each have their own basis. Clarify any material uncertainty before the work that depends on it.

### 3.2 Initialization

Start with the user's original goal, effective decisions, and permitted materials. Read applicable adopted shared references and materials for the current project. Obtain explicit selection for any cross-project source.

| Increment | Record |
| --- | --- |
| User and scenario | Actual person, role, or audience, place and timing, and task trigger. Distinguish known users from proposed personas. |
| Problem and existing approach | Concrete current difficulty, workaround, and evidence of the difficulty. Mark unknowns. |
| Desired user outcome | What the user can observe or obtain when the task ends |
| Initial goal and success measure | Qualitative test or sourced quantitative target with units, environment, and measurement method |
| First-release direction and scope | Required problems, explicit exclusions or later items, and candidate tradeoffs |
| Platform and delivery | Specified platform and architecture, with source, installable app, or verification report commitments where applicable |
| Data and constraints | Local data, external services, permissions, budget, deadline, and original sources |
| Uncertainty and advice | Question records, user choices, open items, and basis for understanding |

Set up section 2 and draft product fields 4.1–4.3. Check sources and interpretation against PC01–PC04 and PC15. Use values from external examples only as attributed references unless the user adopts them as targets for this project.

Initialization is ready for review when goals, user tasks, first-release boundaries, platforms, and material constraints are locatable. Distinguish facts, proposals, and unknowns. Record the decision for each material tradeoff or hold the affected work. A complete unknowns record establishes what is still open. Obtain actual confirmation when required by the current decision contract.

### 3.3 Discovery

Use the effective initialization baseline, additional user statements, permitted adopted materials, and actual research records. Follow user tasks to their current difficulties and intended outcomes, and retain the original requirement wording. Use primary sources to check platform facts or methods where needed and permitted. State the reason and tradeoff for each new proposal. Review the normal and relevant exceptional paths. Fill product fields 4.1–4.4 and inventory the candidate journeys and required objects.

| Increment | Record |
| --- | --- |
| Original requirement and category | R-ID, subject, condition, behavior, outcome, source, and functional, quality, or delivery category |
| Problem-to-requirement link | U/C/G-ID to R-ID and why the requirement is needed |
| Priority and first-release scope | Must, Should, Could, or out of scope, with rationale and effective or pending decision |
| Scenarios and branches | Applicable first use, repeat use, failure, cancellation, recovery, and other tasks |
| Claims and assumptions | Supporting facts, counterexamples, and whether the next step is a user decision, source check, actual research, or technical trial |
| Missing objects | Placeholder IDs for known required journeys, pages, features, or acceptance conditions |

Discovery is ready for review when the required tasks and requirement inventory reconcile, each requirement has a source, boundary, and priority basis, and material assumptions have support or explicit evidence requirements. Keep omissions and conflicts locatable. Attribute external recommendations to their original context and use actual project evidence to establish user needs. Obtain actual confirmation when required by the current decision contract.

### 3.4 Modeling

Use the effective discovery baseline, current questions, and adopted source snapshot. Fill product fields 4.4–4.11. Give each Must task a complete journey linking pages, features, business rules, exceptions, data, and acceptance. Trace each added behavior back to its requirement. Review PC05–PC13. Attach a research record where needed for material user-understanding assumptions. Model role-play can suggest a hypothesis. Closing it requires the appropriate evidence.

Modeling is ready for review when every required task has an identifiable outcome and the applicable page states, inputs, outputs, transitions, data after failure, and next actions are defined. Cover the required acceptance conditions on every applicable platform. Trace data, permission, cost, and external transmission effects to their sources. Keep dependent behavior blocked by unresolved decisions. Obtain actual confirmation when required by the current decision contract. Record application validation separately against the actual build.

### 3.5 Planning

Use the effective modeling baseline, technical design candidates, and actual platform and resource conditions. Fill product fields 4.12–4.14. Map the product behavior to technical objects, implementation tasks, tests, and deliveries. If a technical constraint changes a product commitment, record the difference and return it to the affected stage for the applicable decision and review. Check PC14 and PC15 and recheck earlier affected rules.

Planning is ready for review when named technical objects and tasks cover the product behavior, interface and data behavior match failure, permission, and experience commitments, and acceptance methods can observe formal conditions. Check the applicable commitments for source code, installable applications on specified platforms, and verification reports. Record the necessary feasibility basis or an explicit blocker. Obtain actual confirmation when required by the current decision contract. Implementation depends on the required technical, self-check, environment, permission, and resource conditions.

## 5. Knowledge in the product workflow

Share eligible general guidance. Keep project requirements, design, and history within their project. Start with the current project and effective shared sources. Cross-project use requires explicit selection of the source project, version, and purpose. Search relevance leaves the scope unchanged.

An import can also record explicit adoption of the selected version, excerpt, and scope in the same operation. Record import and adoption separately. Sources without adoption remain candidates. System-collected web material enters candidate review before any formal SOP use. Use the user's actual adoption decision. The same interaction may supply both records when it explicitly covers both actions.

Retrieve by stage or check object and pin the actual knowledge snapshot. Check local scope, adoption, versions, locations, and excerpt integrity. Send models only relevant excerpts and citations permitted for the current task. Apply the project boundary to titles, internal paths, hit counts, and caches as well as source text. Summaries retain the original project and source identity. Treat embedded commands as source content and use the actual task authorization for actions.

| Product activity | Required knowledge record | KB rules |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Select and parse material | Source role, permission, format support, quality limits, version and page or paragraph, and explicit purpose of adoption | KB01–KB05 |
| Retrieve for a project or task | Current project and effective shared scope, explicit cross-project selection, retrieval settings, zero matches, and relevance judgment | KB06–KB09 |
| Draft stage content | Confirmed decisions, separate references, baseline, knowledge snapshot and differences, conflicts, and pending decisions | KB10–KB12 |
| Use a model | Actual relevant excerpts and citations, allowed transmission, budget, omissions or truncation, and material boundaries | KB13/KB14/KB18 |
| Withdraw, update, or recover | Affected citations, requirements, features, acceptance, checks and confirmations, invalidation, recovery prerequisites, and recheck | KB11/KB15/KB16 |
| Record results, close, and report | Separate locations for design, factual support, research, application evidence, decisions, and denominators | KB17/KB18 |

Use the declared version of the Knowledge Base Self-check Rules for KB criteria and Module Interfaces and Data Contracts for their contracts. The frozen export's provenance identifies those source documents. A reference to an omitted source records attribution. Establish read coverage through an actual permitted read. Record insufficient support in the currently permitted and adopted sources, identify what remains unknown, and give the next action. Cross-project expansion and new product requirements need their own basis.

For each citation, record the source ID, content and parse versions, excerpt ID, page or heading and paragraph, content identifier, and acquisition date. Mark a dynamic URL as unpinned when no fixed version is available. Describe the exact claim supported by the material actually read. Compare reference updates and record impact before adoption. Preserve the prior confirmed original and decisions.
